{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}=[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 2t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "