0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t)={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9108dced82c6cb3acf6ef30d87531244a88a01288772cefc5d4d3f20b5e123ab"} {"question" "q-334387" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334387" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{3t}+e^{-3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334387" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-334387" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{3t}+e^{-3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-333713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-333713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-333713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9108dced82c6cb3acf6ef30d87531244a88a01288772cefc5d4d3f20b5e123ab"} {"question" "q-333713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/