Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "q-333713" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\cos(2t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/state.json" "file_sha256" "cae41375d9854e678f9299c1588c33616a29253eb6ae6b40b8702e80bd1963e0"} {"question" "e-21695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/d9bdce761076544aff166409cc78bdf918c4afc2c47b67e6366e3aabb78087b7.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21695.json" "file_sha256" "b3203d080a0e31d56b6afcaab43618f7c70c79007481ab1108d3cb38242fbbff"} {"question" "e-21695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x},$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/bb710709a593f8d185cde601fb5082d7f79ed21af824c28949477d2011479d95.png)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21695.json" "file_sha256" "b3203d080a0e31d56b6afcaab43618f7c70c79007481ab1108d3cb38242fbbff"} {"question" "e-21697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[ \\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$*\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) =  \\big[ A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/be39ec10432eb81c3e00fdf30599353450e1ae7ebebd136f20dce45deb685cac.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/09febb48bb00d22caebf088103181b5db6377befccad18f1370718351cc91e9d.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/999db06dcfe0878ddb3316b11546668ba9442d8fd110165e5076b90964b84905.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21697.json" "file_sha256" "113df1e5efcff375c1a646c09842b8b9972f652c15ac8f797652de9423f80987"} {"question" "e-21697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]\n= A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}.$ From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].$ Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1},$ we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t).$ Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21697.json" "file_sha256" "113df1e5efcff375c1a646c09842b8b9972f652c15ac8f797652de9423f80987"} {"question" "e-21696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/819e3b9672e898801ef7320c1d80246d218ae89e76b3247c86b642a5f5790400.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21696.json" "file_sha256" "bd89c88b0aae61871084febd96babeadb2c1c928862a64820e90d4e20c0c8343"} {"question" "e-21696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/4d6fd69672291bf4c39d33a04656258b89a500bc039588a9fd27eb10db328cc3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/example-21696.json" "file_sha256" "bd89c88b0aae61871084febd96babeadb2c1c928862a64820e90d4e20c0c8343"}] "usage" {"q-333713" [] "e-21697" [] "q-333723" [] "q-334329" [] "e-21695" [] "e-21696" []} "basis" 2513 "retractions" [[17592186221935 :question/problem "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x, x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos 2t \\\\ \\sin 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin 2t \\\\ \\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [17592186221927 :question/problem "Consider the system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t) = Φ(t)c$ for some constant vector $c = [c_{1}, c_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n> $L3$: $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t) = c_{1}x_{1}(t) + c_{2}x_{2}(t)$\n> $L4$: $Φ(t)c = c_{1}x_{1}(t) + c_{2}x_{2}(t)$\n> $L5$: $x(t) = Φ(t)c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line $L1$ by the {{field-3}}."] [17592186221925 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $x^{′} = Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| $Φ_{0}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186221132 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$, shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system."] [17592186221132 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4te^{5t} \\\\ 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} & 4te^{5t} \\\\ 0 & 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order."] [17592186221134 :question/problem "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[\\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.*\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$."] [17592186221134 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}$. Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$. This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n$\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}$. We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n$\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big] = A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}$. From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$. Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1}$, we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t)$. Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1$."] [17592186221133 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system."] [17592186221133 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\ndenoted $\\Phi_0(t)$, is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix.\n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{0} & -7α_{1}t \\\\ 7α_{1}t & α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((7\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(7\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(7\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t) \\\\\nα_{0} + 7\\textrm{i}α_{1}t &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); 7α_{1}t = \\sin (7t)⇒{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); α_{1} = \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{0} &= \\cos (7t) \\\\\n7α_{1}t &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= \\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7t) & -\\sin (7t) \\\\ \\sin (7t) & \\cos (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220528 17592186220529 17592186220530 17592186220531 17592186220533 17592186220534 17592186220535 17592186220536 17592186220538 17592186220539 17592186220540 17592186220541 17592186220587 17592186220588 17592186220589 17592186220590 17592186220591] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220507 :answer-field/id] [17592186220507 :answer-field/key] [17592186220507 :answer-field/type] [17592186220508 :answer-field/id] [17592186220508 :answer-field/key] [17592186220508 :answer-field/type] [17592186220509 :answer-field/id] [17592186220509 :answer-field/key] [17592186220509 :answer-field/type] [17592186220510 :answer-field/id] [17592186220510 :answer-field/key] [17592186220510 :answer-field/type] [17592186220527 :answer-field/id] [17592186220527 :answer-field/key] [17592186220527 :answer-field/type] [17592186220532 :answer-field/id] [17592186220532 :answer-field/key] [1